  "summary": {
    "plain": "Four 12-metre telescopes on a mountain in Arizona that catch the faint flashes of light made when gamma rays hit the atmosphere, letting astronomers study exploding stars, black holes and other extreme places in the universe.",
    "technical": "Array of four 12 m imaging atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes at the Fred Lawrence Whipple Observatory, Mount Hopkins, Arizona (1268 m altitude), sensitive from 85 GeV to over 30 TeV. Proposed 1996, collaboration formed 2000, four-telescope array approved 2003, prototype first light February 2004, full array completed January 2007, with a 2009 sensitivity reconfiguration and 2012 camera upgrade."
  },
